.TH ACCESS 2 
.SH NAME
access \- determine accessibility of file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int access(const char *name, int mode);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Access
checks the given
file
.I name
for accessibility according to
.I mode,
which is 4 (read), 2 (write) or 1 (execute) or a combination thereof.
Specifying mode 0
tests whether the directories leading to the file can be
searched and the file exists.
.PP
An appropriate error indication is returned if 
.I name
cannot be found or if any
of the desired access modes would not be granted.
On disallowed accesses
\-1 is returned and the error code is in
.IR errno .
0 is returned from successful tests.
.PP
The user and group IDs with respect to which permission is checked
are the real UID and GID of the process, so this call is
useful to set-UID programs.
.PP
Notice that it is only access bits that are checked.
A directory may be announced as writable by
.I access,
but an attempt to open it for writing will fail
(although files may be created there);
a file may look executable, but
.I exec
will fail unless it is in proper format.
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
stat(2)
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_access
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
